  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...High pressure and a relatively dry airmass will\nremain in place through the middle of the week, with \ntemperatures forecast to be at, or just below, normal for early \nOctober. The drier airmass plus decent radiational cooling \nconditions may allow some inland locations to dip into the upper\n40s tonight and possibly even again Wednesday night. Closer to \nthe coast, high clouds skirting the area at times may prevent \ntemperatures from getting as low as they otherwise could. \n\nIn addition to the lower temperatures, the decent radiational \ncooling conditions each night will probably favor a risk of fog \ndevelopment. At the moment, it looks to be more shallow in \nnature and not as impactful, but is something we'll continue to \nmonitor.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Late this week and into this weekend, the upper\nlevel pattern will begin to change. Initially, an upper level \nridge will briefly expand across the Southeast U.S. While dry \nweather will continue beneath the ridge, building low-level \nthicknesses should support warming temperatures, with highs \nflipping back to above normal. Most inland areas should manage \nto reach the low to mid 80s, with highs around 80 along the \ncoast. \n\nOver the weekend, and into early next week, the ridge is \nforecast to shift east into the western Atlantic. As this \nhappens, it should help steer any Gulf tropical system north \nthrough the Gulf Coast States and into the TN/OH Valleys. East \nof that system, a warm front is forecast to lift northeast along\nthe Southeast U.S. coast on the leading edge of a plume of \ndeep, tropical moisture. Of note, while a weak area of low \npressure may develop along the northward-advancing warm front, \nthe latest guidance has significantly backed off on the \npotential for a stronger low to develop. Should this trend hold,\nit would mean weaker winds and less coastal impacts. There is \nstill a lot of uncertainty with the track and evolution of the \nGulf system and the track once it moves inland.\n\nFor now, the main messaging focus is for a period of moderate \nto heavy rain on Sunday and Monday across ENC. Ensemble, \ndeterministic, and machine learning guidance are well-clustered \non a broad area of 1-2\" of rainfall for the area. Of note, there\nis a smaller cluster of guidance that suggests amounts as high \nas 2-3\", but for now that is a lower probability solution. It is\nworth noting, though, that the probabilities of exceeding 1\" of\nrain have increased to 50-70% for much of the area. Guidance \nsuggests minimal instability, but sometimes this type of setup \ncan favor some shallow, rotating thunderstorms along the \nadvancing warm front where southeasterly flow is enhanced.",
